Acute Kidney Injury (AKI)
Genitourinary Renal & Mens Health
Sudden oliguria, Rapid rise in serum creatinine, Peripheral oedema

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GRM-001
Reason for Visit: Acute reduction in urine output with progressive peripheral oedema and extreme lethargy, requiring urgent assessment to prevent critical metabolic derangement or fluid overload.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a 24-hour history of severe oliguria, increasing dyspnoea, and gross peripheral oedema. Urgent after-hours assessment was mandated to evaluate for impending pulmonary oedema and life-threatening hyperkalaemia, as delaying until regular in-hours consultation posed an unacceptable risk of irreversible renal or cardiac compromise.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I haven't passed any urine since yesterday morning, my legs feel like heavy water balloons, and I am too exhausted to get out of bed."
History of Present Illness: Symptoms commenced abruptly 36 hours ago following a severe bout of gastroenteritis and poor fluid intake. Initially, the patient noted generalized fatigue and profound lethargy. Within 24 hours, this progressed to sudden onset of oliguria with urine output visibly less than 400 mL per day. Concurrently, peripheral pitting oedema predominantly in the lower extremities developed, alongside mild exertional dyspnoea indicating early fluid overload. Nausea, anorexia, and mild confusion have compounded the presentation. The patient reports a recent history of severe hypovolaemia secondary to gastrointestinal losses and recent initiation of NSAIDs for back pain, which likely precipitated the insult.
Functional Status: Bedbound currently due to severe lethargy and dependent oedema; usually independent.
Social/Safety Concerns: High fall risk secondary to orthostatic hypotension and mild encephalopathy; spouse is increasingly distressed by the rapid cognitive decline.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 98/60 | HR: 112 | Temp: 36.8C | RR: 22 | SpO2: 95% | Wt: 88 Kg
General: Ill-appearing, visibly lethargic, mildly confused but rousable, displaying signs of systemic fluid overload and dehydration concurrently.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Tachycardic with orthostatic hypotension. Respiratory: Bibasal fine crepitations noted on auscultation, consistent with early pulmonary oedema. Abdomen: Soft, non-tender, no palpable bladder to suggest post-renal obstruction. Extremities: 2+ peripheral pitting oedema to the mid-calf bilaterally. Bedside ultrasound/catheterization confirms sudden oliguria with minimal residual volume. Point-of-care testing/expected laboratory trajectory reflects a rapid rise in serum creatinine.
Environmental Observations: Clear pathways to the bathroom, ambient temperature adequately controlled, excessive NSAID blister packs found on the bedside table.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against current prescriptions. High-dose ibuprofen identified as an acute nephrotoxic precipitant alongside prescribed perindopril. Both currently withheld.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Kidney Injury (AKI) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Congestive Heart Failure Exacerbation - Ruled out/unlikely due to the primary history of hypovolaemia/gastroenteritis preceding the oedema, rather than a primary cardiac event.

Acute Urinary Retention - Ruled out/unlikely due to an empty bladder on percussion and absence of suprapubic pain or mass.

Chronic Kidney Disease (End-Stage) - Ruled out/unlikely due to the acute, sudden onset of symptoms in a previously well patient with normal baseline renal function.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, life-threatening cessation of urine production coupled with signs of volume depletion (hypotension, tachycardia) and paradoxically sequestered fluid (pulmonary and peripheral oedema). The recent gastrointestinal losses combined with nephrotoxic medication use strongly indicate a prerenal progressing to intrinsic acute kidney injury. Urgent physical examination was required to rule out post-renal obstruction and to assess the severity of pulmonary fluid overload. Delaying this assessment until the next available in-hours period would have exposed the patient to an unacceptable risk of fatal hyperkalaemic arrhythmias or severe hypoxic respiratory failure from pulmonary oedema. This presentation unequivocally satisfies the criteria for urgent after-hours medical intervention under MBS items 585-600.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediate cessation of all NSAIDs and ACE inhibitors. Intravenous fluid resuscitation initiated cautiously with 250mL normal saline bolus under strict auscultatory monitoring to correct intravascular depletion without worsening pulmonary oedema. Urgent comprehensive metabolic panel, venous blood gas, and formal renal ultrasound ordered.
Positive Behavioral Change: To build a habit of safe medication use, the patient is instructed to place a daily hydration tracker on the fridge. By marking off each glass of water consumed, the patient will receive immediate visual confirmation of adequate hydration, reinforcing the behavior necessary to protect renal perfusion. Furthermore, replacing NSAIDs with paracetamol for pain relief will prevent recurrent nephrotoxic insults.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent pathology domiciliary collection arranged for early morning.
DME/Safety (Optional): Commode placed beside the bed to reduce fall risk during nocturnal voiding attempts.
Follow-up: GP review required within 12 hours for blood result review and clinical reassessment.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department via 000 is required if the patient develops worsening shortness of breath at rest, severe chest pain, profound confusion, or completely fails to pass any urine over the next 6 hours.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This diagnosis is presumptive pending formal laboratory investigations; renal function can deteriorate rapidly.
Clinical Course: With the removal of nephrotoxic agents and careful fluid balancing, renal function often recovers, but the acute phase requires intense monitoring to prevent life-threatening electrolyte disturbances.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call 000 immediately if breathing becomes difficult while lying flat, if you experience chest palpitations, or if confusion worsens. Go to the ED if zero urine is produced in the next 6 hours.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your kidneys have temporarily stopped filtering properly due to severe dehydration and ibuprofen use. Stopping these medications and carefully giving fluids will help flush and restart the kidneys.
Actionable Steps: Drink 200mL of water every hour while awake. Mark it on your fridge chart to build a consistent hydration habit, ensuring your kidneys receive constant, steady blood flow.

